ulty",112,"2026-10-30"],["2607.28956","MerchantBench: Benchmarking LLM Agents for Long-Term Coherence in E-Commerce Operations",85,"2026-08-05"],["2608.09802","SWE-Bench ProMax: Benchmarking Agents on Large-Scale Multilingual Code Refactoring",73,"2026-08-11"],["2608.11341","Apodex Discovery: Reality Benchmarks and Environments for Evaluating and Building Discoverative Artificial Intelligence",73,"2026-08-17"],["2609.33295","TraceDance: An Automated System for Building Agent Behavior Benchmarks from Real-World Agent Deployment Traces",70,"2026-09-29"]],"featured":[{"id":"2610.40627","date":"2026-11-01","title":"ShallowDefend: Harnessing Shallow Safety Alignment for Multi-Turn Defender","tldr":"Training a chatbot to write an explicit, rule-checkable safety decision before it replies turns the shallowness of safety alignment into a defense against multi-turn jailbreaks, without a judge model in the reward."},{"id":"2610.36269","date":"2026-10-28","title":"Cross-modal Steering Vectors Exist Across Both Image and Text Generation","tldr":"In models that generate both text and images with shared weights, a steering vector found only from text, such as happy versus sad, also steers generated images, and image-derived vectors steer text."},{"id":"2610.33512","date":"2026-10-27","title":"Eat Your Precision: Precision Objective Mitigates Reward Hacking in Rubric-based RL","tldr":"Rubric rewards for open-ended RL only measure coverage, so models learn to pad; adding a precision term for necessary, factually correct sentences raises win rate over the base model from 10.6% to 68.0%."},{"id":"2610.34654","date":"2026-10-27","title":"Thinking-in-Bets at Test Time Reduces Sycophantic Revisions in LLM Agents","tldr":"Having an agent privately record its claims, evidence and confidence before reading user feedback sharply cuts sycophantic reversals of correct work while still letting it fix real bugs, without any training."},{"id":"2610.31778","date":"2026-10-26","title":"Benchmarks Are Surprisingly Compaction-Friendly","tldr":"When long-running coding agents summarize their context to keep going, benchmark scores barely change, but the rules they were given quietly disappear from the summaries."},{"id":"2610.28679","date":"2026-10-24","title":"VerifierBench: Benchmarking the Generation of Executable Verifiers for LLM Agents","tldr":"Across 19 models, agent-written verifiers accept valid solutions less reliably than they reject invalid ones, often because they impose unjustified checks that their own self-tests never challenge."}]}